          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-c99d416e0700fb47\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Princess Marya directly avows that she loves Mlle Bourienne “more than ever.” The phrase principally presents her continuing and even increased love as true. Its reassuring and forgiving use does not itself constitute a relational undertaking; the explicit commitment occurs in the following clause about doing everything in her power for Bourienne’s happiness. Marya speaks calmly and deliberately, with no evidence that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The utterance renews reassurance after Bourienne fears rejection, which gives it relational significance, but the commitment force is carried chiefly by the adjoining promise rather than by “Je vous aime” itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Princess Marya directly addresses Mlle Bourienne while comforting her after discovering her with Anatole.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Mlle Bourienne believes she has forfeited Marya’s affection, but Marya responds with a calm, compassionate avowal of increased love. The narration describes Marya’s tender love and pity, and her subsequent thoughts confirm a self-sacrificing desire to secure Bourienne’s happiness. This supports sincerity and forgiving reassurance. The emotional circumstances do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Pourquoi? Je vous aime plus, que jamais” (“Why? I love you more than ever”).\",\"supports\":\"The affirmative present-tense statement, strengthened by “more than ever,” directly avows Marya’s loving state and strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, Marya holds the weeping Mlle Bourienne, gently strokes her head, and looks at her with “tender love and pity.”\",\"supports\":\"The narration independently corroborates the avowed affection and frames the words as compassionate reassurance rather than irony or deception.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Mlle Bourienne says she is “lost forever” in Marya’s heart, making Marya’s reply a direct denial of feared emotional rejection.\",\"supports\":\"The conversational function is reassuring, but it remains primarily an avowal of an existing state rather than an undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marya continues, “et je tâcherai de faire tout ce qui est en mon pouvoir pour votre bonheur” (“and I shall try to do everything in my power for your happiness”).\",\"supports\":\"An explicit commitment is present in the adjoining clause, clarifying that any undertaking force belongs mainly there rather than to the highlighted love formula.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marya speaks while quietly comforting Bourienne and later replies with a sad smile; the narration gives no indication of blurting or involuntary verbal discharge.\",\"supports\":\"Supports E=0 despite the scene’s strong emotion.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the highlighted utterance as a sincere, forgiving avowal. Its reassuring context and the separate accompanying promise can be described without adding core P, E, or O force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The supplied analytical translation was used only to clarify the multilingual source context; the classification is grounded in the supplied canonical text.\"}"
        }
